in listening mode to over a hundred  local government leaders on the  coronavirus front line and we've got sky  with us for the first few contributions  Sky News joined a video call revealing  the challenges of the ongoing lockdown  first the care sector 5010 tests it's  just not happened then on council  finances ministers have been set to us  overtly three or four times  don't worry spend what it takes we will  reimburse you and we started to hear in  the last few days and particularly a  very concerning call with Nick leaders  yesterday on that commitment rapidly on  homelessness and supplies for the  vulnerable many of the food that was  being delivered it was over out of date  or close to being out of date moldy  bread has been being delivered to  residents which clearly is a real  concern and what was the lesson from the  discussion the governor's put a lot of  schemes in place and the question is how  they're being implemented in is there a  gap and we heard about real gaps one of  the starkest that I heard that was  enfield there enfield councils got 5,000  people working in the care sector and  they've only had ten tests opposing  maybe easy  but deciding is difficult does the  Labour leader think that ministers are  going to have to make trade-offs for  instance between young and old well I  don't think we'll be trade-offs as such  what we've got a duty to do is to keep  everybody safe and well that may be  putting in different support for  different sectors of course but it's not  really about a trade on but British  politicians never like to acknowledge  the trade-offs that they make in public  when they come to a decision and we've  seen that time and again from  politicians conservative as well as now  labour but the thing about the  coronavirus can  is that there are so many of them the  state of the economy under lockdown  versus the health of the nation  the young versus the old that's why one  cabinet minister said to me it was going  to take weeks to work out how to  convince the public that it might be  safe to come out of lockdown the chief  medical officer today saying that we are  reaching the peak but that might not be  enough at the moment we are not yet at  the point where we can say confidently  and safely this is now past the peak and  we can start thinking very much about  the next phases tomorrow a meeting of  Cobra likely to decide to continue  lockdown until at least the end of April  life slowing but ministers need to stop  too much coming to a complete halt  Sam Cote Sky News
 

